<article class="enc-article">
<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nikephoros II Phokas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 912–<a href="/years/969">969</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Nicephorus II Phocas</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nikephoros II Phokas was a Byzantine general and emperor from 963 to 969. He reconquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> in 961 after the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraklion">Chandax</a>, sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> in 962 and later took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a>. A patron of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athanasios-the-athonite">Athanasios the Athonite</a>, he backed the founding of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-lavra">Great Lavra</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-athos">Mount Athos</a>. He was murdered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-i-tzimiskes">John Tzimiskes</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nikephoros-ii-phokas.jpg" alt="Nikephoros II Phokas" width="320" height="426" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown Byzantine artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nikephoros_Phokas.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Nikephoros Phokas was born around 912 into the Phokas family, which had produced several generals, among them his father Bardas Phokas, his brother Leo Phokas and his grandfather <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikephoros-phokas-the-elder">Nikephoros Phokas the Elder</a>. He became military governor of the Anatolic Theme in 945 under Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-vii">Constantine VII</a>, and in 954 or 955 he replaced his father as Domestic of the Schools, which put him in charge of the eastern army.</p><p>In 960 a fleet of 308 ships carrying 50,000 troops was assembled against the Muslim Emirate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>, and the minister Joseph Bringas entrusted the expedition to Nikephoros. After a nine-month siege of the fortress town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraklion">Chandax</a> he entered it on 6 March 961 and soon took the whole island. He then marched into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a>, captured Anazarbos and later, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-i-tzimiskes">John I Tzimiskes</a>, entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> on 24 December.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Emperor Romanos II died on 15 March 963. On 2 July the army proclaimed Nikephoros emperor at Caesarea, and once Bringas had been forced to flee <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> he was proclaimed there on 16 August and married the empress Theophano. In the east he completed the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a> in 964 and 965, taking Mopsuestia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarsus">Tarsus</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niketas-chalkoutzes">Niketas Chalkoutzes</a> recovered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>. In the west he was less successful: a fleet sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> was routed and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rometta">Rometta</a> fell, and his fighting with the German emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-i">Otto I</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Apulia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calabria">Calabria</a> brought no lasting gains.</p><p>His popularity rested on his conquests. To pay for the army he cut court spending, curtailed the immunities of the clergy and forbade new monasteries, and heavy taxes and a debased currency provoked riots. He also asked the church to honour soldiers killed fighting the Saracens as martyrs, a demand that proved unpopular. A devout man, he helped his friend, the monk Athanasios, found the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-lavra">Great Lavra</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-athos">Mount Athos</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The plot against Nikephoros began when he dismissed Michael Bourtzes after the disobedience at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a>. According to Joannes Zonaras and John Skylitzes, Theophano and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-i-tzimiskes">Tzimiskes</a> had become lovers and conspired against him. On 11 December 969 Tzimiskes and his entourage entered the bedchamber through a door Theophano had left unlocked and killed him. Tzimiskes took the throne, and a rising of the Phokas family under Nikephoros's nephew Bardas Phokas was quickly subdued. In legends told on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> under Venetian rule he was a point of reference, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kallergis-family">Kallergis family</a>, the most prominent of the island's nobility, claimed descent from him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nikephoros II Phokas” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After <b>Nicephorus II Phocas</b> retook Cilicia from the Arabs in 965 the Byzantines settled Christians from Syria and Armenia there.</blockquote><a href="/empire/armenian-kingdom-of-cilicia">Armenian Kingdom of Cilicia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Emperors of the 10th century protected these holdings: Constantine VII forbade selling military land below a value of four pounds of gold for a cavalryman and two for a sailor, and <b>Nikephoros II Phokas</b> raised the minimum to twelve pounds for his new heavy cavalry.</blockquote><a href="/societies/byzantine-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Byzantine Empire</a></li><li><blockquote>The general <b>Nikephoros II Phokas</b> was crowned emperor, swearing to protect the two sons of the late Romanos II, Basil II and Constantine VIII, who were already co-emperors.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-16">16 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Byzantine emperor <b>Nikephoros II Phokas</b>, who had retaken Crete and Cyprus and conquered Cilicia, was murdered by his nephew, the general John Tzimiskes, who seized the throne as John I.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-11">11 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Co-emperor in name since 962, alongside his father Romanos II, <b>Nikephoros II Phokas</b>, John I Tzimiskes and his brother Basil II, Constantine VIII became sole emperor on Basil's death.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-15">15 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>As Byzantine power revived in the 10th century, <b>Nikephoros II Phokas</b> sent the patrician Niketas Chalkoutzes to retake Cyprus in 965.</blockquote><a href="/state/arab-byzantine-condominium-of-cyprus">Arab-Byzantine Condominium of Cyprus</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Cappadocia, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=38.67,34.84&amp;year=949" aria-label="Cappadocia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5415 776 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Nikephoros II Phokas"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5865 1046h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5865 1046h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=38.67,34.84&amp;year=949">What was here in 949 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
